<!--
    The widened half of the measured libc signature table (`option libcsigs
    on|off`).

    `libcsigs` shipped 206 signatures, chosen by which callee would move the
    type_match metric on the cases a rival decompiler already got right. That
    question leaves out every import the corpus simply uses: the `*at` family,
    the SELinux surface coreutils links against, the account and xattr names.
    A caller that only ever hands its argument to one of those keeps the
    inferred integer, because nothing in the program says what the pointer
    addresses and the callee has no declaration to say it either.

    The selection rule for the widened set is mechanical: an UNDEFINED FUNC
    symbol in at least three of the frozen corpus's 665 dynamically linked
    binaries, not already carried, whose platform declaration reduces WHOLE to
    the width-stable `Ty` vocabulary. Derivation, ranking and the rejected set
    are in docs/features/libcwiden/.

    The XML wrapper only supplies the harness architecture (the `option` command
    needs one before the first `load file`). Both passes clear it and load the
    same real ELF.

    The fixture's four wrappers each pass a `const char *` parameter to exactly
    one of the new imports (`faccessat`, `renameat`, `mkdirat`, `chroot`) and do
    nothing else with it, so the parameter's type comes from the callee's
    declaration or from nowhere. Pass 1 (`option libcsigs off`) reproduces the
    bug and pass 2 is the default; the callees are named in both passes, so the
    only thing that moves is the signature. The gate is an Architecture flag read
    at the DEFERRED analysis commit, so the option command sits between
    `load file` and `read symbols` - after `clear architecture` it is back at its
    default, which is what makes pass 2 the shipped arm with no command at all.

    Source: decompiler/crates/kuna-analysis/tests/fixtures/libcwiden_x86_64.c
-->
